title: Adding one
mode: vhdl
chapter: Buses and numbers
check: behaves
---- intro
Adding 1 is so common it gets its own lesson: every counter and every program step does it. A number can be added straight onto an `unsigned`: `unsigned(a) + 1`.

With 4 bits, 15 + 1 wraps round to 0.
---- task
Make `y` the 4-bit number `a` plus one.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;
use ieee.numeric_std.all;

entity inc4 is
  port(
    a : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
    y : out std_logic_vector(3 downto 0)
  );
end inc4;

architecture rtl of inc4 is
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;
use ieee.numeric_std.all;

entity inc4 is
  port(
    a : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
    y : out std_logic_vector(3 downto 0)
  );
end inc4;

architecture rtl of inc4 is
begin
    y <= std_logic_vector(unsigned(a) + 1);
end rtl;
---- hint1
Read `a` as a number, add 1, turn it back into bits.
---- hint2
`y <= std_logic_vector(unsigned(a) + 1);`
---- solution
`y <= std_logic_vector(unsigned(a) + 1);` Set `a` to 1111: `y` is 0000.
---- done
Wire builds this from a row of half adders. Soon you will put it in a loop with a register, and it will count.
